\nQ 44.34375 8.109375 44.34375 7.71875 \nQ 44.046875 6.0625 40.71875 2.78125 \nQ 37.40625 -0.484375 34.375 -0.484375 \nQ 30.28125 -0.484375 27.734375 8.015625 \nL 25.484375 15.625 \nL 19.34375 4.984375 \nQ 16.109375 -0.59375 11.140625 -0.59375 \nQ 7.8125 -0.59375 5.46875 1.375 \nQ 4.6875 2.734375 4.6875 3.90625 \nQ 4.6875 5.375 5.703125 6.390625 \nQ 6.734375 7.421875 7.8125 7.421875 \nQ 9.078125 7.421875 10.6875 5.90625 \nQ 12.3125 4.390625 13.671875 4.390625 \nQ 14.84375 4.390625 16.3125 6.9375 \nL 24.03125 20.21875 \nL 20.015625 33.296875 \nQ 19.046875 36.625 17.390625 36.625 \nQ 15.921875 36.625 14.25 35.109375 \nQ 12.59375 33.59375 11.921875 32.328125 \nQ 11.53125 32.328125 10.9375 33.109375 \nQ 10.359375 33.890625 10.359375 34.28125 \nQ 10.640625 35.9375 13.953125 39.203125 \nQ 17.28125 42.484375 20.3125 42.484375 \nz\n\" id=\"CrimsonText-Italic-120\"></path>\n     </defs>\n     <g transform=\"translate(273.750468 244.798528)scale(0.15 -0.15)\">\n      <use xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Italic-120\"></use>\n     </g>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"line2d_1\">\n    <defs>\n     <path d=\"M 0 3 \nC 0.795609 3 1.55874 2.683901 2.12132 2.12132 \nC 2.683901 1.55874 3 0.795609 3 0 \nC 3 -0.795609 2.683901 -1.55874 2.12132 -2.12132 \nC 1.55874 -2.683901 0.795609 -3 0 -3 \nC -0.795609 -3 -1.55874 -2.683901 -2.12132 -2.12132 \nC -2.683901 -1.55874 -3 -0.795609 -3 0 \nC -3 0.795609 -2.683901 1.55874 -2.12132 2.12132 \nC -1.55874 2.683901 -0.795609 3 0 3 \nz\n\" id=\"me321077b63\" style=\"stroke:#000000;\"></path>\n    </defs>\n    <g clip-path=\"url(#p9817aa21ea)\">\n     <use style=\"stroke:#000000;\" x=\"59.339103\" xlink:href=\"#me321077b63\" y=\"223.119103\"></use>\n     <use style=\"stroke:#000000;\" x=\"104.278045\" xlink:href=\"#me321077b63\" y=\"131.198541\"></use>\n     <use style=\"stroke:#000000;\" x=\"149.216986\" xlink:href=\"#me321077b63\" y=\"112.814428\"></use>\n     <use style=\"stroke:#000000;\" x=\"194.155928\" xlink:href=\"#me321077b63\" y=\"94.430316\"></use>\n     <use style=\"stroke:#000000;\" x=\"239.09487\" xlink:href=\"#me321077b63\" y=\"57.662091\"></use>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"line2d_2\">\n    <path clip-path=\"url(#p9817aa21ea)\" d=\"M 59.339103 223.119103 \nL 62.220959 212.809927 \nL 65.102815 203.272822 \nL 67.984671 194.467975 \nL 70.866527 186.356508 \nL 73.748383 178.900475 \nL 76.630239 172.062862 \nL 79.512095 165.80759 \nL 82.393951 160.099509 \nL 85.275807 154.904405 \nL 88.157663 150.188996 \nL 91.039519 145.920932 \nL 93.921375 142.068795 \nL 96.803231 138.602103 \nL 99.685087 135.491303 \nL 102.566943 132.707776 \nL 105.448799 130.223838 \nL 108.330655 128.012734 \nL 111.572743 125.819251 \nL 114.814831 123.903069 \nL 118.056919 122.230329 \nL 121.659239 120.617934 \nL 125.62179 119.094984 \nL 129.944574 117.672798 \nL 134.987822 116.245896 \nL 142.192462 114.465214 \nL 156.601742 110.957786 \nL 162.725686 109.193807 \nL 168.129166 107.395444 \nL 173.172414 105.470035 \nL 177.85543 103.443514 \nL 182.538446 101.171177 \nL 187.221462 98.644041 \nL 191.904478 95.859623 \nL 196.587494 92.821947 \nL 201.630742 89.279593 \nL 207.034222 85.196635 \nL 212.797934 80.555541 \nL 219.642342 74.743586 \nL 230.08907 65.531754 \nL 239.09487 57.662091 \nL 239.09487 57.662091 \n\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-linecap:square;stroke-width:2.3;\"></path>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"p9817aa21ea\">\n   <rect height=\"260.82\" width=\"262.488358\" x=\"26.679727\" y=\"7.2\"></rect>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for graph of a curve\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>In quadrant 1:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>The curve begins at point (0 comma 1).</li>\n<li>The curve rises sharply to point (2 comma 6).</li>\n<li>The curve rises gradually to point (4 comma 7).</li>\n<li>The curve rises gradually to point (6 comma 8).</li>\n<li>The curve rises sharply and ends at point (8 comma 10).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The graph shows the momentum <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math>, in newton-seconds, of an object <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> seconds after the object started moving, for <math alttext=\"0 less than or equals x less than or equals 8\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#8804;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>8</mn></math>. What is the average rate of change, in newton-seconds per second, in the momentum of the object from <math alttext=\"x equals 2\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"x equals 6\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>6</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["0.5","1/2"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"one half\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. For the graph shown, <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> represents time, in seconds, and <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> represents momentum, in newton-seconds. Therefore, the average rate of change, in newton-seconds per second, in the momentum of the object between two <em>x</em>-values is the difference in the corresponding <em>y</em>-values divided by the difference in the <em>x</em>-values. The graph shows that at <math alttext=\"x equals 2\"><mrow>\n<mi>x</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, the corresponding <em>y</em>-value is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>. The graph also shows that at <math alttext=\"x equals 6\"><mrow>\n<mi>x</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>6</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>, the corresponding <em>y</em>-value is <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>. It follows that the average rate of change, in newton-seconds per second, from <math alttext=\"x equals 2\"><mrow>\n<mi>x</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"x equals 6\"><mrow>\n<mi>x</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>6</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"StartFraction 8 minus 6 Over 6 minus 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to&nbsp;<math alttext=\"two fourths\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"one half\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 1/2 and .5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","externalid":"71a3b42d-82b2-44f5-8a6d-50461b5cbd2f","correct_answer":["0.5","1/2"]},"createDate":1691007959834,"updateDate":1691007959834}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959834, 1691007959834),
    ($SATQ$9bedc4a0$SATQ$, $SATQ$1d0e4fb4-a269-44f0-b4ed-6434ddb20c94$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A company that produces socks wants to estimate the percent of the socks produced in a typical week that are defective. A random sample of <math alttext="310"><mn>310</mn>
</math> socks produced in a certain week were inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn>
<mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in this week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext="3.62 percent sign"><mn>3.62</mn>
<mo>%</mo></math>. Based on the estimate and associated margin of error, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion about all socks produced by the company during this week?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"731ba114-e385-4e4a-9bf4-4963a9882e4c","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5","content":"<p>It is plausible that between <math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"c8159a84-b91d-4636-910b-8f269201e006","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"54d86e2c-b735-43d5-868d-baa9c0e31fda","content":"<p>It is plausible that more than <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that, based on the sample, an estimate of <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in a certain week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext="3.62 percent sign"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. This estimate, plus or minus the margin of error, gives an interval of plausible values for the actual percent of all socks produced by the company that week that are defective. Subtracting <math alttext="3.62 percent sign"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> from <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext="8.38 percent sign"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Adding <math alttext="3.62 percent sign"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> to <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext="15.62 percent sign"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Therefore, it is plausible that between&nbsp;<math alttext="8.38 percent sign"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext="15.62 percent sign"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company are defective.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> is the estimated percent of defective socks based on the sample. However, since the margin of error for this estimate is known, the most appropriate conclusion is not that the percent of defective socks is exactly <math alttext="12 percent sign"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;but instead that it lies in an interval of plausible percents.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9bedc4a0","external_id":"1d0e4fb4-a269-44f0-b4ed-6434ddb20c94","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"534821ee-4e9d-4491-9bf1-bc9036034bea","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.F.","skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A company that produces socks wants to estimate the percent of the socks produced in a typical week that are defective. A random sample of <math alttext=\"310\"><mn>310</mn>\n</math> socks produced in a certain week were inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in this week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math>. Based on the estimate and associated margin of error, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion about all socks produced by the company during this week?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"731ba114-e385-4e4a-9bf4-4963a9882e4c","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5","content":"<p>It is plausible that between <math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"c8159a84-b91d-4636-910b-8f269201e006","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"},{"id":"54d86e2c-b735-43d5-868d-baa9c0e31fda","content":"<p>It is plausible that more than <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>"}],"keys":["14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that, based on the sample, an estimate of <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in a certain week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. This estimate, plus or minus the margin of error, gives an interval of plausible values for the actual percent of all socks produced by the company that week that are defective. Subtracting <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> from <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Adding <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> to <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Therefore, it is plausible that between&nbsp;<math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company are defective.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> is the estimated percent of defective socks based on the sample. However, since the margin of error for this estimate is known, the most appropriate conclusion is not that the percent of defective socks is exactly <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;but instead that it lies in an interval of plausible percents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357405,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"9bedc4a0","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1730147357405,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"1d0e4fb4-a269-44f0-b4ed-6434ddb20c94","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"534821ee-4e9d-4491-9bf1-bc9036034bea","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>","id":"731ba114-e385-4e4a-9bf4-4963a9882e4c"},{"content":"<p>It is plausible that between <math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>","id":"14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>","id":"c8159a84-b91d-4636-910b-8f269201e006"},{"content":"<p>It is plausible that more than <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of the socks are defective.</p>","id":"54d86e2c-b735-43d5-868d-baa9c0e31fda"}],"externalid":"1d0e4fb4-a269-44f0-b4ed-6434ddb20c94","keys":["14166eff-ddff-46b2-bcc9-c791a5701cd5"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that, based on the sample, an estimate of <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in a certain week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. This estimate, plus or minus the margin of error, gives an interval of plausible values for the actual percent of all socks produced by the company that week that are defective. Subtracting <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> from <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Adding <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> to <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> yields <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Therefore, it is plausible that between&nbsp;<math alttext=\"8.38 percent sign\"><mn>8.38</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"15.62 percent sign\"><mn>15.62</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company are defective.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math> is the estimated percent of defective socks based on the sample. However, since the margin of error for this estimate is known, the most appropriate conclusion is not that the percent of defective socks is exactly <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;but instead that it lies in an interval of plausible percents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A company that produces socks wants to estimate the percent of the socks produced in a typical week that are defective. A random sample of <math alttext=\"310\"><mn>310</mn>\n</math> socks produced in a certain week were inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext=\"12 percent sign\"><mn>12</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all socks produced by the company in this week are defective, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.62 percent sign\"><mn>3.62</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math>. Based on the estimate and associated margin of error, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion about all socks produced by the company during this week?</p>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1730147357405,"updateDate":1730147357405}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357405, 1730147357405),
    ($SATQ$9bf4c545$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$10225-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.G.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Evaluating statistical claims: Observational studies and experiments $SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">The members of a city council wanted to assess the opinions of all city residents about converting an open field into a dog&nbsp;park. The council surveyed a sample of 500&nbsp;city residents who own dogs. The survey showed that the majority of those sampled were in favor of the dog park. Which of the following is true about the city council&rsquo;s survey?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>It shows that the majority of city residents are in favor of the dog park.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>The survey sample should have included more residents who are dog owners.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>The survey sample should have consisted entirely of residents who do not own dogs.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>The survey sample is biased because it is not representative of all city residents.</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The members of the city council wanted to assess opinions of all city residents. To gather an unbiased sample, the council should have used a random sampling design to select subjects from all city residents. The given survey introduced a sampling bias because the 500 city residents surveyed were all dog owners. This sample is not representative of all city residents because not all city residents are dog owners.<p>Choice A is incorrect because when the sampling method isn&rsquo;t random, there is no guarantee that the survey results will be reliable; hence, they cannot be generalized to the entire population. Choice B is incorrect because a larger sample of residents who are dog owners would not correct the sampling bias. Choice C is incorrect because a survey sample of entirely non&ndash;dog owners would likely have a biased opinion, just as a sample of dog owners would likely have a biased opinion.</p><p>
        <!--EndFragment-->
      </p></p>
