    ($SATQ$9ce8cbbe$SATQ$, $SATQ$8758bc63-f1f1-4ad8-90b7-562482615452$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Probability and conditional probability$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Set K consists of all the positive integers that are less than or equal to <math alttext="160"><mn>160</mn>
</math>, where none of the integers are repeated. If a number is selected at random from set K, what is the probability of selecting a number that is even and less than or equal to <math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d48f4297-9da1-464c-9cc2-ffd90c862062","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five thirty seconds\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>32</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d2b7543b-0f75-4cd4-aa21-66954cab8a2e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"three sixteenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mn>16</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"96f58baf-2a90-45a2-ae68-2516feae2552","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five twenty firsts\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>21</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"eadf4353-c393-4879-a77c-ebd63d5fedee","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five sixteenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>16</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d48f4297-9da1-464c-9cc2-ffd90c862062"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. It's given that set K&nbsp;consists of all the positive integers that are less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="160"><mn>160</mn></math>, where none of the integers are repeated. Therefore, set K&nbsp;consists of the integers <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1"><mn>1</mn></math> through <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="160"><mn>160</mn></math>, which is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="160"><mn>160</mn></math> numbers. Of these, the integers <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1"><mn>1</mn></math> through <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math> are less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>. Therefore, there are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math> numbers that belong to set K&nbsp;that are less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>. Of these, half are even. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 50 Over 2 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>50</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="25"><mn>25</mn></math>, numbers that belong to set K&nbsp;are even and less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>. The probability of selecting at random a number from set K&nbsp;that is even and less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math> is equal to the number of numbers that belong to set K&nbsp;that are even and less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>, which is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="25"><mn>25</mn></math>, divided by the total number of numbers that belong to set K, which is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="160"><mn>160</mn></math>. It follows that the probability of selecting at random from set K&nbsp;a number that is even and less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 25 Over 160 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>160</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="five thirty seconds"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting at random a number from set K&nbsp;that is less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>, not a number that is even and less than or equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="50"><mn>50</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9ce8cbbe","external_id":"8758bc63-f1f1-4ad8-90b7-562482615452","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"92e67d28-d2cd-4376-a3ad-58060942274c","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.E.","skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>Set K consists of all the positive integers that are less than or equal to <math alttext=\"160\"><mn>160</mn>\n</math>, where none of the integers are repeated. If a number is selected at random from set K, what is the probability of selecting a number that is even and less than or equal to <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d48f4297-9da1-464c-9cc2-ffd90c862062","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five thirty seconds\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>32</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d2b7543b-0f75-4cd4-aa21-66954cab8a2e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"three sixteenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mn>16</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"96f58baf-2a90-45a2-ae68-2516feae2552","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five twenty firsts\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>21</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"eadf4353-c393-4879-a77c-ebd63d5fedee","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five sixteenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>16</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["d48f4297-9da1-464c-9cc2-ffd90c862062"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. It's given that set K&nbsp;cons