$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["3","540"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is 3,540. According to the table, of 400 voters randomly sampled, the total number of men and women who plan to vote for Candidate A is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/9ee22c16/img_000_2999fd6e.png" alt="202 plus 34 equals 236"></span>. The best estimate of the total number of voters in the town who plan to vote for Candidate A is the fraction of voters in the sample who plan to vote for Candidate A, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/9ee22c16/img_001_ab08b4fa.png" alt="the fraction 236 over 400"></span>, multiplied by the total voter population of 6000. Therefore, the answer is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/9ee22c16/img_002_ed609fc5.png" alt="open parenthesis, the fraction 236 over 400, close parenthesis, times, 6000, equals 3540"></span>.</p>
